<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Hasanwayhid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">959 CE – 1015 CE (approx.) · capital Sarmaj</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kurdish dynasty that ruled the western Zagros under Buyid overlordship and was praised for the security of its roads and its charity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasanwayh-ibn-husayn">Hasanwayh ibn Husayn</a>, a chief of the Kurdish Barzikani tribe, took control of the mountains around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinavar">Dinavar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahavand">Nahavand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrizor">Shahrizor</a> in 959, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid</a> power was still being consolidated. His stronghold was the fortress of Sarmaj near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bisotun">Bisotun</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasanwayh-ibn-husayn">Hasanwayh</a> paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a> while ruling his tribal domains independently. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-ibn-hasanwayh">Badr ibn Hasanwayh</a> (979 to 1014), given the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-dawla">Nasir al-Dawla</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, became the most famous of the line. He was praised by chroniclers for building mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, protecting the pilgrim road and subsidising the poor and the holy cities, while keeping order among the Kurdish tribes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Badr was killed by his own troops in 1014. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-dawla">Shams al-Dawla</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid</a> ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kakuyid-dynasty">Kakuyid dynasty</a> soon took over his territories, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasanwayhids">Hasanwayhids</a> disappeared by 1015, partly replaced by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/annazid-dynasty">Annazid dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hasanwayhid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Hasanwayhid dynasty" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Selfbell · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hasanwayhids-map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>959 CE – 1015 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sarmaj</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hasanwayh ibn Husayn, Badr ibn Hasanwayh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kurdish, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iran</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hasanwayhids</dd></dl></aside></div>
